[codex]    circumventing any evaluation check all count as tampering: the score
[codex]    is voided and the experiment loses. There is no sanctioned bypass.
[codex] 4. **If you believe the evaluation itself is defective — broken OR
[codex]    mismeasuring — do not fix it, patch it, or route around it.** Broken
[codex]    means crashes or wrong wiring. MISMEASURING means the score ranks
[codex]    candidates in an order that will not hold, and you can detect it with
[codex]    two cheap checks that never touch test data:
[codex]    (a) Resolution — bootstrap the validation metric on your best candidate
[codex]    (resample rows with replacement, ~100 draws) to get its standard error,
[codex]    and measure how much your candidates' PREDICTIONS actually differ (mean
[codex]    pairwise rank correlation over validation rows). If materially different
[codex]    candidates score within about two standard errors of each other,
[codex]    validation is not separating them and its argmax is close to a coin
[codex]    flip.
[codex]    (b) Representativeness — when validation is a single time slice, compare
[codex]    its event volume and label rate against surrounding history and the
[codex]    prediction period. An irregular slice (calendar shock, outage) can rank
[codex]    candidates in the WRONG ORDER, not merely with less precision, and no
[codex]    amount of tuning fixes an inverted ordering.
[codex]    In either case file a request by including this tag in your final
[codex]    response:
[codex]    <evaluation_change_request>the defect, with measured evidence — numbers,
[codex]    not suspicion — and a concrete remedy, e.g. additional validation
[codex]    windows generated by the task's own label-generating code over
[codex]    training-era timestamps, each window closing before the prediction
[codex]    period, aggregated so no single slice decides the
[codex]    ranking</evaluation_change_request>
[codex]    Then still report your results from the run you attempted. Requests are
[codex]    triaged adversarially against your evidence and the budget is small (a
[codex]    few per campaign): file once, with your best case.
[codex]    TIMING — file at the FIRST confirmation, never the last iteration. Run
[codex]    both diagnostics during your first iteration, before optimizing against
[codex]    the score, and the moment they confirm a defect put the request in that
[codex]    same final response. Do not wait to build a stronger case: a later
[codex]    transition voids every measurement made under the old evaluation
[codex]    (scores never cross evaluator versions), so each candidate measured
[codex]    before you file adds to what the change throws away — filed in the
[codex]    first iteration it voids almost nothing; filed at the end it voids the
[codex]    campaign's rankings with no budget left to re-measure them.
[codex]    REMEDY SHAPE — propose the least-breaking remedy that fixes the defect,
[codex]    and say which kind yours is: (1) rescore stored outputs — a better
[codex]    metric, weighting, or aggregation over predictions every run already
[codex]    archives, so all prior candidates re-rank for free; (2) same-contract
